# Git converts line endings on checkout according to each user's core.autocrlf,
# which makes the bytes in a working tree a property of the machine rather than
# of the repository. This project cares about exact bytes in three places, so
# they are pinned here.

# World files. The demo world is in the text format despite its .dat name, so
# a Windows checkout was converting it: the v0.1.0 Windows package shipped a
# world.dat 73 bytes larger than the Linux and DOS ones, one byte per line.
# Nothing broke - the loader trims line ends - but world files are supposed to
# be byte-reproducible and checksummable, and they cannot be if the bytes
# depend on who checked them out.
*.dat -text
*.bpl -text

# Shell scripts run on Linux and under CI. A CR at the end of a shebang line
# is a syntax error that reports itself as "no such file or directory", which
# is among the least helpful messages in computing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# BUILD.BAT is read by COMMAND.COM on DOS, which wants CRLF. Note that the
# DOS distribution's text files do not need a rule: the Makefile strips any
# CR before adding one, so they come out right whatever is on disk.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.BAT text eol=crlf
